Alcohol-Induced Diuresis
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Renal Loss
Post-consumption polyuria, Morning extreme thirst, Dark concentrated morning urine

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-004
Reason for Visit: Urgent request for medical evaluation regarding severe dizziness, intractable headache, and palpitations following an evening of alcohol consumption, requiring assessment to rule out cardiac arrhythmias or severe hypovolemia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an inability to stand without near-syncope and severe headache, raising concerns for significant intravascular volume depletion. This required an urgent after-hours home visit to assess hydration status and safely guide oral fluid resuscitation prior to morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Severe headache, dizziness, and intense thirst. Patient states, My mouth is like sandpaper, my head is pounding aggressively, and the room spins when I try to stand up.
History of Present Illness: Following a period of heavy alcohol consumption last night, the patient experienced transient marked polyuria, characterized by increased urinary frequency shortly after drinking. This was followed by a sleep period of four hours. Upon waking, the patient experienced morning extreme thirst, a severely dry mouth, and passed dark concentrated urine. The patient now reports orthostatic dizziness, lightheadedness, and a severe throbbing headache associated with volume depletion. Fatigue and general malaise are prominent.
Functional Status: Currently resting in bed; mobility is impaired due to orthostatic dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Mild risk of syncope-related falls; roommate is present to provide fluids and assistance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78.0 Kg
General: Appears tired, lethargic, resting in a supine position, rubbing temples due to headache.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Mild tachycardia, normal heart sounds, regular rhythm. No murmurs. Mild orthostatic drop in blood pressure without total syncope. Respiratory: Clear bilaterally. Gastrointestinal: Abdomen soft, non-tender. Genitourinary/Hydration: The diagnostic triad is historically and clinically supported by post-consumption polyuria, morning extreme thirst, and the patient passing dark concentrated morning urine just prior to arrival. Examination reveals dry oral mucosa, normal or slightly reduced skin turgor, and lack of peripheral edema, confirming renal fluid loss.
Environmental Observations: Room is dark and quiet, appropriate for headache management.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No concurrent diuretic medications or anti-hypertensives complicating the clinical picture.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Alcohol-Induced Diuresis - Status: Stable but symptomatic
Differentials:

Acute Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, or infectious contacts.

Diabetic Ketoacidosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: No history of diabetes, normal respiratory pattern (no Kussmaul breathing), and no fruity odor to breath.

Migraine Headache - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear association with alcohol intake, generalized severe dehydration signs, and lack of specific aura or unilateral throbbing typical of their historical migraines, though alcohol is a known trigger.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Alcohol acts as a potent inhibitor of antidiuretic hormone (ADH), leading to profound obligatory renal free-water loss. The patient's presentation of severe orthostatic lightheadedness, tachycardia, and a severe dehydration headache confirmed significant intravascular volume depletion. Delaying care until the next in-hours period would have left the patient at risk for a syncopal fall or escalating distress from the intractable headache. Urgent after-hours assessment confirmed the hemodynamic stability of the patient, ruled out more sinister causes of secondary tachycardia, and allowed for the initiation of a structured, aggressive oral rehydration strategy to safely resolve the hypovolemia. The expected investigation results include transient low urine osmolality followed by increased urine specific gravity the following morning that would assist in confirming the diagnosis, though testing is not immediately required.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Paracetamol 1000mg PO for headache management. Directed the immediate consumption of 500ml of an isotonic oral rehydration solution, followed by steady water intake.
Positive Behavioral Change: To correct this dehydration safely, commit to drinking 250ml of an electrolyte solution every hour for the next four hours. Set an alarm on your phone for each hour; when the alarm sounds and you finish the glass, check it off a physical list. The visual progress and the immediate reduction in your headache severity will reward your consistent effort, establishing a direct link between structured fluid intake and symptom relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up only if symptoms fail to resolve entirely within 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or call 000 if you experience a complete loss of consciousness, if you develop severe chest pain or palpitations that do not settle with resting and hydration, or if you begin vomiting persistently and cannot keep any fluids down.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis assumes uncomplicated physiological diuresis secondary to alcohol; underlying conditions can alter recovery.
Clinical Course: The kidney's ability to concentrate urine will return as alcohol is metabolized. With appropriate oral fluid replacement, symptoms will resolve linearly over the next 12 to 24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Stay seated or lie down while drinking your fluids to prevent fainting. Stand up very slowly in stages over the next few hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Alcohol temporarily turned off the hormone that tells your kidneys to save water, causing you to pee out your vascular volume. We are replacing that specific volume now with electrolytes.
Actionable Steps: Drink 250ml of fluid every hour. Use a timer. Stand up slowly.

